Afterschool Pascal Runtime Library Exception
============================================

Additional permission under section 7 of the GNU General Public License,
version 3, granted for `runtime/pasrt.c`, `runtime/pasrt.h`, and the library
built from them (`libpasrt.a`) — together, "the Runtime Library".

    As a special exception, if you link the Runtime Library with other files
    to produce an executable, that does not by itself cause the resulting
    executable to be covered by the GNU General Public License. This
    exception does not however invalidate any other reasons why the
    executable file might be covered by the GNU General Public License.


Why this exists
---------------

The Runtime Library is linked into *every* program this compiler builds: it
carries the formatted output, the file machinery and the run-time checks that
ISO 7185 requires, none of which can be expressed in the emitted IR. Without
this exception, compiling an ordinary Pascal program with `pascalcc` would put
that program under the GPL, which is not the intent — a compiler should not
decide the licence of what it compiles.

The same reasoning is why GCC ships the GCC Runtime Library Exception, and why
GNAT and libstdc++ carry exceptions of their own. This one is the simpler,
long-established linking-exception wording rather than GCC's, because this
runtime has no notion of eligible compilation processes or target code to
qualify.

**The compiler itself is not covered by this exception.** `selfhost/compiler.pas`,
`tools/pascalcc`, the build files and everything else in this repository are
under the GNU General Public License version 3 or later, with no additional
permissions. Modifying the compiler and distributing it means distributing your
changes under the same terms.

**Nor does it grant anything about the compiler's *output* beyond linking.**
The IR `pascalc` writes is derived from your source, not from the compiler, so
it was never covered in the first place.
